If you want to talk about your mother instead of your grandmother, it's actually more natural and relatable for IELTS. Here's a Band 7+ version:

I'd like to talk about my mother, who is the person I know that enjoys cooking for others the most. Ever since I was a child, she has been the one preparing meals for our family, and cooking has always been one of the ways she expresses her love and care.

My mother is a hardworking and caring person. Despite having a busy schedule, she still makes time to cook for the family almost every day. She especially enjoys preparing traditional Vietnamese dishes such as braised pork with eggs, sour soup, and fresh spring rolls. On special occasions like birthdays, family gatherings, or Lunar New Year, she often spends hours in the kitchen preparing a large meal for everyone.

I think she likes cooking because it brings her joy to see other people enjoying her food. Whenever relatives or guests visit our house, she always insists on preparing something special for them. For her, food is more than just a necessity; it's a way of bringing people together and creating a warm and welcoming atmosphere.

Personally, I really appreciate her cooking. Not only is the food delicious, but every meal reminds me of the effort and love she puts into taking care of our family. Whenever I'm away from home, one of the things I miss most is her home-cooked food. In fact, no restaurant meal can truly compare to it.

Overall, my mother is someone who genuinely enjoys cooking for others, and her meals have created countless happy memories for our family. That's why I admire her so much and feel incredibly grateful whenever I have the chance to enjoy her cooking.
